WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.530
Thank you for joining
us for our hot topics.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.550
Today, we're very lucky
to have Professor Michael

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.650
Lynn from Stanford.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:24.020
Michael received his AC summa
cum laude in biochemistry

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.280
from Harvard.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.660
And he followed that with a PhD
in biological and biomedical

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.800
sciences, also at Harvard
at the medical school.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.780
He also received an MD
degree from UCLA in 2004.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:44.850
Dr. Lin is currently a
professor at Stanford,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.320
where he leads a
research group developing

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.620
protein-based tools for
imaging and controlling

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.630
biomedical processes.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:59.120
He has a long string of awards.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.930
And to me, most importantly,
he is the author

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.750
of what I consider as
the most viral slide deck

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.590
on COVID-19 in the recent days.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.760
Michael, welcome.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:11.750
Welcome to CSAIL.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:12.920
Welcome to a MIT.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.350
Welcome to the College
of Computing at MIT.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.310
We're eager to learn from you.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.480
Please go ahead.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:18.980
Oh, great.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:19.480
Hello.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.500
Well, thank you
very much, Daniela.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.500
Thanks, everyone, for
taking your time to join.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.900
It's my pleasure to share with
you what I've learned myself

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.650
about the virus and the
disease in the last few weeks.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.200
So you know, we're living
through unprecedented times.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.950
We've seen nothing
like this since perhaps

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.130
the 1918 flu epidemic.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.760
And I think it's useful for
us to all learn the facts

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.790
and try to spread knowledge
about the disease.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.860
So I'm gonna get started and
share my PowerPoint window.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.570
So some of you have
seen this slide deck.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.610
Some of you have not.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.890
And I understand
this slide deck,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.080
I had made for my
own lab meeting

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.850
to basically ease anxiety
among my lab members

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.170
and use the facts to try to
put together a plan of action.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.490
And so, now I'm happy to share
these facts with you as well.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.030
And I'll try to explain--

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.400
I'll try not to get too deep
into the biochemical details,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.770
but hit the most important
points along the way.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.500
So as some of you
have seen, this

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:34.640
is not a pretty PowerPoint.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.550
I haven't had the time
to make it really glossy.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.950
It's not like a TED talk.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.300
I'm not pushing a simple idea.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.190
There's just a lot of facts.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.680
But I think it's only
when you understand

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.140
all the facts together
that things make sense.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.080
So we'll just get started
with some very general numbers

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.120
to put some context
into the numbers we're

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.590
going to get into shortly.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.750
The total population of
USA is close to 330,000.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.237
I've been doing a lot
of my calculations

00:03:03.237 --> 00:03:05.820
based on numbers in California
because it's just easier for us

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.280
to think about it locally.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.570
That's about 1/8 of the
USA, 40 million people.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.750
So, you know, the
major causes of death

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.780
for young people in the
US are traffic accidents.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.110
There's 30,000 per year
in the USA, about 3,000

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.490
for California.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.900
For older people, actually flu
is the leading cause of death.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.285
There are 40,000 deaths a year.

00:03:26.285 --> 00:03:27.660
It's never really
known how much,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.480
but the range this
year is estimated

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.960
between 22,000 and 55,000.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:40.830
So out of that 40,000,
about 5,000 in California.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.930
So for a population of
40 million in California,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.880
you can use this sort of number
to get a general idea of how

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.140
lethal flu is.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.450
If 25% of people got the flu
virus and only 5,000 died,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:04:00.380
that's an infection fatality
rate or IFR of 0.05%.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.840
It's probably actually more
like 0.1%, that is more

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.440
like 12% of the population might
get the flu virus in a given

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.880
year because we have flu shots.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.090
So is everything OK?

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.410
You guys can see the
slides, Daniella?

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.089
OK.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.760
Yes, everything is going great.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.600
Thank you very much.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:20.100
Yes.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.250
OK, great.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:26.780
So we'll get right into
some quick definitions.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.720
The disease name is COVID-19.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.070
I think we have all seen this
term much more than we want.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.100
Technically, it refers to
the disease, not the virus.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.400
In practice, the term
"cases of COVID-19"

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.740
are used to refer to
having a positive lab test,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.080
whether or not
you have symptoms.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.230
For reasons that are
partly political, partly

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:51.520
medical, if you just have
the symptoms, you know,

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.950
you're not necessarily
yet a COVID-19

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:58.340
case because you could have,
for example, a bad case of flu.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:02.670
So you tend to wait for
the lab test results.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.870
The WHO, the World
Health Organization,

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.320
introduced the name COVID-19
after holding the world

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.930
in suspense for a few
weeks, saying how important

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.180
it was to get the name right.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.822
And then when they
announced the name,

00:05:19.822 --> 00:05:21.280
there was the name
for the disease,

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.440
but there was no
name for the virus.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.990
And so everyone
started using COVID-19

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:30.130
as the name for the virus
because there was no other name

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.610
given by WHO for it.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.410
So this has led
to some confusion.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.470
Some allowed people use the term
COVID-19 to refer to the virus.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.650
I mean, I guess that's fine in
a popular lay point of view.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.200
But technically, it actually
makes a difference because when

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:43.950
we--

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:48.390
I'd like to talk about cases
of the disease and infection

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:50.700
numbers based on the
estimate of the virus.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.120
And so I try to use
a different name

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.020
for the virus when possible.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.320
I mean, I personally think
COVID-19 is a terrible name.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.390
Because it stands for
"coronavirus disease 2019."

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.390
And who says, if you want
to talk about a virus,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.610
you could call it
the COVID-19 virus.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.280
That would mean
coronavirus disease

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:15.150
2019 virus, which is a
mouthful for saying nothing.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.900
It basically reveals
that the disease name

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.540
has no informational value.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.030
So previously, we
had named diseases

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.640
by some sort of
description as a disease.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.160
So SARS was named
SARS because it

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.910
stood for severe acute
respiratory syndrome.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.070
And actually this
disease is very

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:44.710
similar to SARS, both in
symptomology and in the agent

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.220
that causes it.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.720
So the World Health Organization
could have just named

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:51.460
this SARS 2 and
that would have been

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.310
both accurate and descriptive.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.080
So what is the
name of the virus?

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.780
It was initially
called 2019-nCOV.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.128
That stands for 2019
new coronavirus.

00:07:04.128 --> 00:07:05.920
And it was given by
some infectious disease

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.960
organization.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.690
This hasn't been picked up
because it's a really hard term

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:11.350
to remember.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.600
You don't usually
think of numbers

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.150
when you think of naming things.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.430
So I think it's also
misleading because it

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:23.140
gives the impression that
this virus is actually novel.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.680
It's not particularly novel.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.490
And I would say it's the least
novel of the new viruses we've

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:30.220
isolated and sequenced.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.510
The most interesting thing about
it is that it's not that novel.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.680
And that's because it's
96% identical to the virus

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.550
that causes SARS in 2003.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:45.830
So in fact, the
biological name for it,

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.370
the name for its sequence
is SARS coronavirus 2,

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:50.950
or SARS-CoV-2.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.900
So we'll use this name
throughout my presentation.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:08:02.140
And I like this name because
it reminds us that SARS-CoV-2

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.408
is very similar to SARS-CoV-1.

00:08:04.408 --> 00:08:05.950
And so a lot of the
things that we've

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:10.390
learned about the coronavirus
one, which came out in 2003,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.360
it's almost 20
years ago, is very

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.450
relevant to predicting
what would happen

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:20.348
with SARS-CoV-2 and COVID-19.

00:08:20.348 --> 00:08:22.390
So I'll just say, if you
haven't realized it yet,

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.660
that I'm not a big fan of how
WHO has managed this crisis.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:29.920
But WHO decided not to
name the virus SARS-CoV-2

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.789
for precisely this
reason, because it made

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.340
people think of SARS.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.710
They wanted to obscure the
relationship between the two

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:39.340
viruses.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:44.570
They thought it would be unfair
to call the virus SARS-CoV-2

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:49.570
and to call the disease SARS-2
because it would remind people

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.260
about SARS.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.170
Actually, I think that would
have been a good thing to do.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.380
And as scientists,
it's better to be

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.720
clear about what you're working
with, rather than trying

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.900
to create false distinctions.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.030
So I call SARS-CoV-2.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.330
So what our coronavirus
is, what our SARS-CoV-2,

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.040
I don't have a picture.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.960
I mean, I think you've seen many
EM images, many illustrations

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:16.950
of the virus.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.960
Like other viruses, it
looks like a little round

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.488
ball with little spikes
sticking out of it.

00:09:21.488 --> 00:09:24.030
From the molecular biology point
of view, what's interesting,

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.280
important to know
is it's fully RNA.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.418
It doesn't go
through a DNA phase

00:09:29.418 --> 00:09:30.960
and that we're
actually very familiar

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.510
with this class of viruses
because they account for 10%

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.270
to 30% of the common cold.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.100
And they're unusually
stable for RNA viruses.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.603
So we're used to hearing
that RNA viruses mutate

00:09:41.603 --> 00:09:42.145
very quickly.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.950
RNA viruses will always be
less stable than DNA viruses.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.130
But among RNA viruses, these
seem to be pretty stable.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:58.860
For example, the coronavirus
OC43 causes the common cold.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.800
It was sequenced
once in the 1960s.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.350
It was sequenced from an
isolate from the 1960s.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.410
And it was sequenced again
from an isolate in 2001.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:09.870
And those only had two
amino acid differences,

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:12.690
six nucleotide differences.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.200
So that's pretty amazing.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.790
As you've probably heard, there
are many coronaviruses in bats.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:20.840
And they seem to hop
easily between species.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.440
So they're quite adaptable to
a variety of mammalian species.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.490
So we had the Middle East
Respiratory Syndrome,

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.430
MERS virus, that hopped
from camels to humans.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.910
The original SARS virus
hop from bats, probably

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.780
to civets and then humans.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:40.220
And then this one most likely
came from bats, as well,

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.040
through some
intermediate animal.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.470
And there's a bat virus
that is very closely

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.030
related to the SARS virus.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:54.280
You can see that in
the bottom red arrow.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.680
And I made this point
that the human cold virus

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.815
OC43 is very closely related
to this mouse hepatitis virus.

00:11:02.815 --> 00:11:04.190
Actually, what's
not showing here

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.680
is there's also a
cow virus that's

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.030
very closely related to humans.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:13.340
So either humans or cows seem
to have given laboratory mice

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:14.150
hepatitis.

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:21.860
So how might you
kill this virus?

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.857
Everyone wants to know
how do you kill it.

00:11:24.857 --> 00:11:25.940
So it's an envelope virus.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.440
It has a thin plasma
membrane that's

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.050
only two molecules
of lipid wide.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.360
So that's actually
really easy to kill.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.710
Anything that would dissolve
a lipid will kill the virus.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:40.400
That includes soaps or
detergents, solvents,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.600
organic solvents like
ethanol, isopropanol.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.350
I like to use Windex
which is a detergent.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:47.900
And bleach will kill it because
it just kills off proteins

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.960
and biological molecules.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.920
So it's also sensitive
to temperature.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.460
I mean, well, I should say
that the original SARS was

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.130
sensitive to temperature.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:03.340
So the current SARS-2
is likely as well.

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.418
And there's some numbers
that you can look at.

00:12:05.418 --> 00:12:06.960
For example, on the
bottom left, this

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:11.380
is a graph of how much
tighter reduction you get

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:12.670
with different temperatures.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.070
Oh, I left my
temperature numbers off.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:20.780
That's actually, I
think, 37, 33, and 28.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.030
I'll have to put that back on.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.045
And then the survival of the
virus depends on the surface.

00:12:28.045 --> 00:12:30.420
So this is a paper that was
recently published in the New

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:32.410
England Journal of Medicine.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:38.310
On smooth surfaces
like steel and plastic,

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:44.140
it survives for longer
than on porous materials

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:46.600
like cardboard.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:50.320
So that means on paper
towels or napkins,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.640
the survival should be
like cardboard or lower.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:56.140
But also the viruses will get
trapped in these paper fibers.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.520
So it's not easy to get virus
out of a napkin, for example.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:03.520
And then it can be killed by UV.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.740
So the amount of UV
in sunlight probably

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:16.030
is enough to kill SARS-CoV-2
by 90% after about three hours.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.640
At the current--

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.220
Michael, could I
ask you a question?

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:19.720
Yes.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:23.980
So I heard that the virus
can survive up to nine days.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:27.183
The data here does not
take us to nine days.

00:13:27.183 --> 00:13:27.850
Can you clarify?

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.680
Yeah, the data here
takes us to three days.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:34.845
So most headlines will say virus
can survive up to three days.

00:13:34.845 --> 00:13:36.220
Yeah, thank you
for the question.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.980
You can see here on the
stainless steel and plastic

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:40.540
that there's--

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.645
the dashed line is the
detectable amount of virus.

00:13:42.645 --> 00:13:44.800
So you could see
that at 72 you still

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.100
are a little bit
above detection.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.140
And then at 96,
it's undetectable.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:54.520
So a lot of places, a
lot of news headlines

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.050
will say three days.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:14:02.390
That's true, that's a
three logged reduction,

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:04.180
so that's 1,000 fold.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.680
I think it's fair to use--

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:09.460
It depends what you want to
use, 10-fold or 100-fold.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.650
I use 10-fold because I
think your transmission

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:17.020
risks are significantly lower
once you've had a 10-fold drop.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.678
So I have a follow-up
question, if you don't mind.

00:14:19.678 --> 00:14:20.470
Yes, please ask it.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:25.060
So we know that there is this
shortage of masks in hospitals.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.250
So if we look at
this data, does that

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:30.040
mean that if we save the
masks for four days, then

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:31.460
they can be reused?

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:34.540
Uh, yes, correct.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.570
That's right.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:40.310
You know, they'll accumulate
dust and other things,

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:43.270
but as long as you can breathe
through them easily, in theory,

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.030
you can just set aside the
mask and wait four days

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:47.290
and pick it up again, yes.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:55.030
OK, so should I move on?

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:56.380
Yes, please.

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:59.920
All right, yeah, so now that
we know how to kill the virus,

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:03.190
we might ask, how
does SARS kill us?

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:06.580
So as many of you know, you
get initial flu-like symptoms,

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:09.820
sore throat, cough, fever, and
aches, shortness of breath.

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:13.270
And for many, in fact,
probably most people,

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:15.845
they only have these symptoms.

00:15:15.845 --> 00:15:18.370
The symptom onset is 48 days.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.980
So it starts about a
week after infection.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:24.230
In severe cases, it will
spread to the lungs and cause

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:24.740
pneumonia.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.157
And that's shown
in the top image.

00:15:26.157 --> 00:15:30.260
So this can be diagnosed by CT.

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:32.580
There's a wide variety
in symptom severity.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:37.680
So about 50% are estimated
to be asymptomatic.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:41.160
That's based on data
from the Diamond

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.990
Princess, the ship in Japan.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:48.900
5% will require hospitalization
and supplemental oxygen

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.200
due to difficulty breathing.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:55.590
And that 5% is taken
from sort of estimates

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:59.250
based on diagnosed cases
of 10% elsewhere adjusted

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:07.530
for the 50% rate of
asymptomatic presentations.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.940
Michael, we have some
questions, if I may interject.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:17.820
So one question is, is the
disease severity related

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.490
to the dose of virus exposure?

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:21.990
Yeah, that's a
really good question.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:23.430
I suspect it is.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:24.990
I haven't seen any data.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:28.430
Because, obviously,
you can't dose people

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.180
with different
amounts of viruses.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.190
But I suspect it is, perhaps.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:36.650
What might happen is if you
get a lot of virus at once,

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:39.290
you'll have more immediate
infection in the lungs.

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:42.430
And you'll just start off
with a higher viral titre.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:45.860
And then your immune system
is behind in the race.

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:48.740
And so an example
this is the doctor

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:50.300
who raised the alarm in Wuhan.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:54.960
His name is Li
Wenliang, I think.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:17:00.035
And, yeah, it's funny.

00:17:00.035 --> 00:17:00.910
It's such a sad case.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.490
Every time I say his
name, I start crying.

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:07.220
Because he was only
39 and he died.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:08.868
So that was really unusual.

00:17:08.868 --> 00:17:10.910
He was treating multiple
patients with the virus.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:15.569
So it probably was because he
was breathing a lot of virus.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:19.680
OK, a few more
questions, and Michael.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.180
So does one maintain
immunity to the virus

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:26.520
if they go through it once?

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:27.298
Probably.

00:17:27.298 --> 00:17:28.840
I mean, there's no
data that suggests

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:29.970
that you lose immunity.

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:34.280
So if it's like any
other virus, then it's

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.830
likely that you are immune.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:40.910
I mean, SARS patients
have proven to be immune.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:44.720
So I think we should work
with that assumption here.

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:50.120
Also, how long
after the infection

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:51.660
will you test positive?

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:55.100
In other words, you are infected
today, you show symptoms,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:56.840
let's say, eight
days from today.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:01.650
At what point will
you test positive?

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.300
Yes, you will
probably test positive

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:07.860
around four days, five days.

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:09.690
Yeah, so these are
great questions.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.880
So what happens in the
course of the disease

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.430
is the virus will
land in your throat

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.140
and it'll start replicating.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:20.560
And it's now a race between your
immune system and the virus.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:24.950
So the virus might double
or it might-- well,

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.130
it's not doubling.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:29.340
It probably has a burst
size of several thousand,

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:32.680
meaning one cell can put out
several thousand viruses.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:38.050
It will do that over a maybe 18
to 24 hours, so about one day.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.590
So the virus is
replicating really quickly.

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:42.330
For you to get a
positive PCR test,

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:46.050
you need to have a swab
that gets enough virus that

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:50.790
creates enough nucleic acid
in the reaction for the test

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:51.610
to detect it.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:54.390
So my guess is
that you will need

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:57.150
to have the virus for
three to four days

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:00.120
before you have a positive test.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.760
I will have one more question,
and then we'll pause.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.190
There are so many
questions on the chat.

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:06.630
I will ask you
one more question.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.610
We'll come back
to other questions

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:12.010
later so you can go on
with your presentation.

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:15.870
And so the question is what
is known about the disease

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.020
severity--

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:22.230
Hey, Jill, I was just reading
a question and I lost it.

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:23.148
One second, sorry.

00:19:23.148 --> 00:19:24.690
It might have been
the question, what

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:26.148
is known about its
disease severity

00:19:26.148 --> 00:19:29.425
with respect to MHC alleles?

00:19:29.425 --> 00:19:31.050
What is known about
why some people are

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.080
more or less susceptible
to catching the disease?

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.910
Yeah, right, that's
a good question.

00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:41.210
So some of it might have to do
with MHC alleles, as described.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:44.580
Although-- Right, so
yeah, it could be that.

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:50.390
It could be differences
in innate immunity.

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:53.540
So your body's first
reaction to the virus

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:57.650
is to produce
cytokines and then have

00:19:57.650 --> 00:19:59.930
cells that are infected
with the virus actually shut

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.540
down protein synthesis
so that viruses

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:04.560
can't reproduce as quickly.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.910
So it's kind of like a
fire alarm, the first step.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:12.770
That might differ
between people.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:14.490
You know, you wonder
why kids don't have

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:15.740
severe effects from the virus.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:21.590
So perhaps kids have a stronger
innate immunity response.

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:26.690
And it's only a few days
later that your T and B

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:32.500
cells are activated
in the antibodies

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:35.600
are produced by your B
cells in sufficient number

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:39.910
to block the spread
of the virus.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:42.020
So the fact that some
people come through it

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:44.090
without any symptoms
at all, like kids

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:48.450
and like 50% of the population
in general, my guess is it

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:53.090
has to do with differences
in innate immunity.

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:56.060
And then how effective
you are in having

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:58.820
your adaptive immunity, so
that's your T cells and B

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:03.260
cells, that specifically
recognize the proteins

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:06.680
on the virus, how
successful you are

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:10.798
in getting those working
in clearing the virus may

00:21:10.798 --> 00:21:11.965
have to do with MHC alleles.

00:21:11.965 --> 00:21:16.650
It may have to do with how many
white blood cells you have.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:22.710
It certainly seems to be more of
a problem as people get older.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:23.600
Thank you.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:24.360
We'll pause.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:27.310
We'll come back to questions
after we hear more of the talk.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:27.810
Go ahead.

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:28.850
Sure.

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:34.120
Yeah, so when it gets
really bad is when you

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:35.560
need mechanical ventilation.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.990
And that's about half the cases
that require hospitalization

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:40.360
begin with.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:44.410
And then, you know, when
people die of the disease,

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:47.140
it's due either to
direct breakdown

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.960
of the alveoli in the lungs--

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:53.110
and there's a photo of
that in the bottom right--

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.990
or you have this massive
release of cytokines

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:57.040
by your immune cells.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:00.020
Because now you've recruited
billions of immune cells

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:00.925
to your lungs.

00:22:00.925 --> 00:22:02.780
And they're all trying
to fight the virus.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.890
And they release a lot of
cytokines, which actually

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:12.610
can cause low blood pressure and
multi organ failure, especially

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:14.730
kidney failure.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.770
So, you know, this is a slide
deck that I had put on the web.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:23.420
And I have links for
all the facts, just

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:26.650
to make sure the facts
are double checked

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:27.780
and for more information.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:34.700
So if you go to my Twitter feed,
which is MichaelZLin, all one

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:39.027
word, Z as in
zebra, MichaelZLin,

00:22:39.027 --> 00:22:39.860
It's a pinned tweet.

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:42.860
You can download the slide
deck from there in the future

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:45.175
if you want to browse
through any of these things.

00:22:45.175 --> 00:22:47.300
So yeah, it doesn't make
for a pretty presentation,

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:51.870
but I think it makes for a
more informative document.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:53.650
All right, so now a lot of text.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:57.340
So what everyone wants to know
is, what is the probability I

00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:58.250
will get the disease?

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:01.060
Like, how many people
have the disease.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.310
And that's impossible to
know from looking at the case

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:03.830
numbers.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:08.320
So this is something
where the news

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:10.440
will report the case numbers.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:11.350
It's misleading.

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:13.850
The case numbers are always
less than the infection numbers.

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:20.110
So if you want to know
infection numbers,

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.490
I think we have to go by--

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:27.220
We have to estimate the
probability of death

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:29.520
and work back from there.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:31.610
So how do we know the
probability of death

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:34.150
from infections?

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.040
Well, you could start
with some good data.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:39.290
So you could start
with a place that

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:43.150
has done the most testing per
capita, and that's South Korea.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:45.955
So right now in South Korea--

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:53.180
well, what you can see in
this graph in the middle

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:56.650
is the number of cases
diagnosed in orange.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:58.170
And the number of
deaths is in red.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:01.310
So it looks like there's
a small number of deaths.

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:03.560
But actually, we know
that deaths, on average,

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.740
lack diagnosis by two weeks,
and this means infections

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:07.450
by three weeks.

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:09.220
It's actually two to
three weeks or infections

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:10.820
by three to four
weeks, with diagnosis

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:13.460
tends to happen around
one week after infection

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:16.100
when the symptoms first start.

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:21.380
So if you look at the
death and case curves,

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:25.670
the death curves indeed lag the
case curves by about two weeks.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:29.670
So this case curve, as you can
see, is sort of flattening out.

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:33.920
It's reaching sort of a constant
rate of about 100 per day.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:39.005
But the death curve looks like
this curve from three weeks ago

00:24:39.005 --> 00:24:40.880
or two weeks ago, which
is right around here.

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:45.370
So it's still sort of in
this inflection point.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.750
It doesn't yet flattened out.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:50.770
That's the death curve
I just mentioned.

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.520
So the current
total deaths occur

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:59.900
from cases diagnosed
two weeks earlier ago.

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:07.390
So if we have 75 total
deaths last week and out

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:10.120
of 4,000 cases
two weeks earlier,

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:14.750
that's a case fatality
rate of about 1.9%.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:17.330
But we know that infections
have to be at least

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:20.730
greater than the number
of diagnosed cases.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:23.180
So that means the true
infection fatality

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:24.470
rate is less than 1.9%.

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:26.720
And it just depends on what
fraction of the infections

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:31.850
were diagnosed at, you
know, two weeks ago.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:35.050
So it's somewhere below 1.9%.

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:37.740
Can I ask you something
about that, Michael?

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:38.410
Mm-hm.

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:42.010
I've been watching the Johns
Hopkins real-time cases

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:43.240
website.

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:47.560
And they report that
for the resolved cases,

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:49.628
the rate is more like 10%.

00:25:49.628 --> 00:25:50.920
How should we think about that?

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:53.830
The closed cases
are the cases that

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:59.080
ended either in the patients
being fully recovered

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.510
or in death.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:03.475
Yeah, well, I think they're
just slow to resolve the cases.

00:26:10.508 --> 00:26:12.550
If you have a severe case,
it takes about a month

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.980
to fully recover
free of symptoms.

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:19.550
And, you know, these
resolution numbers,

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:20.800
I'm not sure how they're done.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.980
They might be waiting for
people to be free of symptoms.

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:25.460
But maybe they just
don't follow up

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.580
in the large
proportion of cases.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:30.400
My guess is they're
not following up.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:33.910
It's just people are too
busy dealing with new cases

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:37.100
to worry about what
happened to the old ones.

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:39.860
So yeah, the case
closure numbers,

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:43.280
resolution numbers have been
low for all the countries.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:50.420
So how else can we get this IFR,
this all important infection

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:51.290
fatality rate.

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:56.732
So an analysis of Chinese
data gave an IFR of 0.5%.

00:26:56.732 --> 00:26:58.690
Some people doubt the
reliability of that data.

00:26:58.690 --> 00:26:59.940
I haven't looked at it myself.

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:02.950
But the analysis of
the Diamond Princess,

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:06.310
that's the ship in
Japan again, gave 1.2%

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:09.460
as the infection fatality rate.

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:11.290
Now, on the Diamond
Princess, some patients

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:16.952
may have been helped with this
new medication remdesivir.

00:27:16.952 --> 00:27:18.910
But also those passengers
were on the old side.

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:22.340
And we know old people
are more impacted.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:28.360
So I think that 1.2 is
sort of a good estimate.

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:31.310
Also, you could look at
population distribution.

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:34.480
So if you look at the
number of people over 60,

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.620
there's more in
Korea than in the US.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.500
And there's more in
the US than in China.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:47.860
So I would guess that the IFR
for the American population

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:50.580
is probably around 1%.

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:52.990
So an estimate based on age
adjusted data from China

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:56.230
also gave a very
similar number, 0.9%

00:27:56.230 --> 00:27:58.840
in the range of 0.4 to 1.4.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:01.270
So the range shows
you how difficult

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:02.732
it is to really pick a number.

00:28:02.732 --> 00:28:03.815
But let's just go with 1%.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:10.630
All right, so we'll
move to the next slide.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:11.760
Oh, I need to click.

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:13.410
OK.

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:16.040
So as everyone knows,
death is much more likely

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:17.490
for the older population.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.670
So that's
unfortunately the case.

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:23.810
It's not that, you know,
the same is true with flu.

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:26.720
So it's not a surprising fact.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:29.450
But you can see that,
and like the flu,

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:31.910
it seems to be much worse.

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:35.660
Now, this again, I
think, is actually

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:39.350
death rates out of
total diagnosed cases,

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.330
without a two or
three week time lag.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:48.090
So this might be
actually a little bit

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:51.990
of an underestimation,
meaning that the denominator

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:57.480
cases might in actuality be
smaller than is shown here.

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:01.600
So this makes the disease
much more severe than the flu.

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:04.530
Michael, we have an
interesting question.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:05.030
Right.

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:07.870
Is there any reason
beyond age distribution

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:12.810
why IFRs would be
different across countries?

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:16.230
So we've heard that in the
US, mostly younger people are

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:19.290
experiencing severe disease.

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:23.140
Yeah, some young people
experience severe disease.

00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:25.920
But I think it's still a small
proportion out of the total

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:27.480
infected in the young people.

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:35.820
I haven't heard of good data
for differences in the age

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:40.090
distribution of severity.

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:43.360
There certainly will be some
country-to-country data.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.510
In countries where older people
are more likely to smoke,

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:50.050
they ought to be more impacted.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:53.770
So maybe that's a little bit
of a difference, like maybe

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:57.760
in South Korea older people
have a smoking history,

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:00.400
whereas that's less
likely in the US.

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:04.390
But I think we lack
the data on that.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:08.790
Also, can the virus mutate
into being more deadly?

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:11.490
We've heard that maybe
there are two strains

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:15.990
and there are worrisome
numbers in France.

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:19.120
It's not clear if
there are more strains.

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.660
What can you share about that?

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:25.170
Yes, it can mutate to be
more deadly, in theory.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:26.910
There's no particular
reason to think

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:29.220
that it will get selected
for that, though,

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:35.780
meaning, well, viruses
actually don't want to kill

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:38.030
their hosts too quickly.

00:30:38.030 --> 00:30:40.100
They'd rather the host
stay healthy and just

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:42.110
be coughing a lot.

00:30:42.110 --> 00:30:47.570
So there's no reason,
in particular,

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:51.190
to be very afraid
of that yet, right?

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:53.170
So meaning, the virus
is known to be stable.

00:30:57.580 --> 00:31:01.390
It's known to adapt to a
bat host in a stable manner.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:04.180
I don't think there's any
reason that it would be selected

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:07.240
for increased fatality.

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:14.035
So right, so I made an
estimate of how many new cases.

00:31:17.223 --> 00:31:18.890
We don't have to go
through the numbers.

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:21.182
But basically you
take the current--

00:31:21.182 --> 00:31:22.850
I split it up into weeks.

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:24.360
I go week by week.

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:27.080
I take the number of
deaths in the last week

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:32.000
and I multiply by 100 to
get a number of infections

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:38.360
that happened in the week
ending four weeks ago.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:42.440
And I do this at the end of each
week to update the slide deck.

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:45.230
So this smooths out
day-to-day differences

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:47.660
in the reporting of deaths.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:49.550
And it also doesn't
require any calculus.

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:51.440
It's basically a
numerical analysis.

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:55.707
It's like doing Riemann sums
with a time interval of a week.

00:31:55.707 --> 00:31:58.290
So what you need to know is sort
of the rate of new infections

00:31:58.290 --> 00:31:58.790
each week.

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:02.430
So if there were, for
example, in California,

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:04.590
there were 10 this week,
10 deaths this week.

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:10.170
That means there were 1,000
new cases four weeks ago.

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:13.740
With a doubling time of the
infection of seven days--

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.910
somebody people have
estimated six days.

00:32:15.910 --> 00:32:18.720
I just used seven so we could
sort of make the analysis

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:19.620
simpler.

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:25.170
With a doubling time of seven
days, then from four weeks ago,

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:30.480
we're now 16-fold higher in
the rate of new infections.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:32.290
There are probably
16,000 new infections

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:39.227
in the last week in California,
which is one in 2,500 people.

00:32:39.227 --> 00:32:41.810
And then in the Bay Area, we're
more impacted in other regions

00:32:41.810 --> 00:32:42.480
of California.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:47.770
So I just assume its one
in 1,000 in the Bay Area.

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:50.540
Now, what can you
do when, you know,

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:52.980
you're in Boston, which
is also heavily impacted?

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.920
So it might be also something
like one in 1,000 people just

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:57.880
got the virus in the last week.

00:32:57.880 --> 00:32:59.740
So what does that mean?

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:02.380
Well, 50% of people
will be asymptomatic.

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:04.390
So they'll never know.

00:33:04.390 --> 00:33:07.635
And this is based on, again,
the Diamond Princess data.

00:33:07.635 --> 00:33:09.510
It's also based on some
mathematical analyses

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:14.580
of the China data and of data
from passengers on evacuation

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.460
flights back to the US.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:22.020
So 50% people will never know.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:23.640
The average incubation
is seven days,

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:27.210
but the transmission may begin
two days before symptoms.

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:29.280
So that means a large
portion of people

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:32.420
will start transmitting
the virus without knowing,

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:35.840
even if they do
eventually get symptoms.

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:40.850
So that means you have
a risk of one in 1,000

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:44.990
from direct personal
contact, perhaps, and higher

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:48.620
than that from touching objects
that are commonly touched,

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:49.910
which are called fomites.

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:51.620
Those are things
like door handles.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:58.068
And the risk of contracting
it from touching fomites

00:33:58.068 --> 00:34:00.110
is in proportion to the
number of people touching

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:01.730
that between cleanings.

00:34:01.730 --> 00:34:04.730
So you know, if 100
people touch that door

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:07.130
handle before you
touch it, now that risk

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:10.380
is increased to 1 in 10.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:11.880
So that means we
have to take action

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:14.580
to reduce the risk of acquiring
the virus, either directly

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:17.699
or from touching.

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:20.550
So what would happen
if we did nothing?

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:24.300
So obviously, exponential growth
will continue with a doubling

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:25.560
time of one week.

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:28.210
And you can do a quick,
simple model as shown here.

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:29.760
This is very amateur model.

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:31.610
Most of you can
do better models.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:33.110
Again, I didn't
want to do calculus,

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:35.350
so there are no equations.

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:36.719
It's just doubling.

00:34:36.719 --> 00:34:39.659
And then we reach a point where
we run out of people to infect.

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:43.920
And according to this
model, that's in late May.

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:47.580
And California officials
estimate 56%, you know,

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.010
total infections.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.750
Angela Merkel famously said 70%.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.837
So it's something
between 50% and 70%.

00:34:54.837 --> 00:34:56.670
At that point, the virus
runs out of targets

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.250
and it's harder for the virus
to find new people to infect.

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:02.580
Everyone else has been immune.

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:06.440
So that makes the
top infection rate

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:08.910
about 20% for the
last two weeks.

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:10.470
And then four weeks
after that, we'll

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:14.430
have deaths that result from
that 20% infection rate.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:16.650
And that's huge.

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:21.090
So if you accumulated all the
deaths, those numbers shown

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:24.240
here in California, you have
to multiply by 8 to get the US.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:27.360
That would be 2 million
cumulative deaths.

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:29.408
Again, this is really
just an amateur, back

00:35:29.408 --> 00:35:30.575
of the envelope calculation.

00:35:30.575 --> 00:35:33.090
But I think it's better than
other back of the envelope

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:37.320
calculations that you've seen
because it uses reported death

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:41.010
numbers and this estimated IFR.

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:44.730
Professional models say 100
million cumulative infections.

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:50.850
That's 30% or you could say
56% in the case of the one that

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:53.530
was done for California.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:57.440
An estimated IFR of 0.5 as
a conservative estimate.

00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:01.580
So I think my estimate
is not too far off.

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:05.330
It might be the worst case
scenario would be the 60%

00:36:05.330 --> 00:36:06.150
and the 2 million.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:08.980
But it could be better.

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:11.230
And there's some nice sites
for following case numbers

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:13.300
and fatality
numbers if you want.

00:36:13.300 --> 00:36:17.020
So compared to the flu, which
had a cumulative infection

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:23.430
rate of 27% and
an IFR of 2%, this

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:27.150
is probably similar
in total numbers.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:31.650
But the flu was
famously devastating.

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:34.650
The disease itself is probably
worse now than the flu.

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:38.190
Because back in 1918, they
didn't have ventilators

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:39.540
or drugs or antibiotics.

00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:47.020
So this is why we need to
famously flatten the curve.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:53.510
There are an estimated 5%
will require hospitalization.

00:36:53.510 --> 00:36:58.490
And 2.5% of those will
require ICU and ventilation.

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:01.090
And the average hospital
stay is three weeks.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:07.630
So that means that in the
last three weeks of May,

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:13.030
we'll get 10%, 20%, and 20%
of the population infected.

00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:15.100
And then that four
weeks later in June,

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:20.440
will results in 1.6 million, 3.2
million and 3.2 million people

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:21.580
needing hospitalization.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:23.110
And due to the three
weeks that they

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:24.610
need to stay in the
hospital, that's

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:27.700
eight million overlapping
at the same time,

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:29.290
compared to one
million hospital beds.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:32.440
So that means we should slow
down the doubling time from one

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:34.450
week to at least eight weeks.

00:37:34.450 --> 00:37:36.190
Of course, the
slower the better.

00:37:38.747 --> 00:37:40.330
So that makes the
next month critical.

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:42.210
And that's why California
is shutting down

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:43.975
and Boston, I think,
has shut down.

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:52.110
Because we need to know how far
down we can get the R0, which

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:55.050
is the reproduction
number or how much

00:37:55.050 --> 00:37:56.910
we can increase
the doubling time.

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:01.530
And due to the delay between
infection and diagnosis

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:03.960
and hospitalization, it
will take us several weeks

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:06.430
to know if it's really working.

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:12.370
And the hard deadline for
this is really early May.

00:38:12.370 --> 00:38:13.870
Actually, I should
update the slide.

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:16.840
I think it's no longer May 16th.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:19.898
I think-- let me go back.

00:38:19.898 --> 00:38:21.565
I guess you could say
it's around May 15

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:25.000
that it would still be bad.

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:27.920
But, you know, it won't
require martial law yet.

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:31.210
But if you got to
May 15th, you would

00:38:31.210 --> 00:38:35.110
have to basically confine
people to their homes

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:41.620
by threat of arrest because
otherwise any further infection

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:43.540
would overflow our
medical system.

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:49.250
So what can flatten the curve?

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:58.370
So I'm optimistic that
weather will help.

00:38:58.370 --> 00:39:01.930
It's not gonna help a lot, but
it's gonna help a little bit.

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:08.680
So based on that graph
from earlier, a 10 degree

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:13.930
rise in Fahrenheit will
increase the doubling time,

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:18.110
will lower the amount of
virus survival by two-fold.

00:39:18.110 --> 00:39:22.600
So I think it should--

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:27.960
I would assume it would increase
the doubling time two-fold.

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:31.190
So actually, I gotta
correct myself.

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:35.180
A 10-degree change, I
think, increases the amount

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:36.480
of death tenfold of the virus.

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:37.520
So I think it should
increase the doubling

00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:38.625
time at least two-fold.

00:39:38.625 --> 00:39:40.500
And in this, you'll also
get people outdoors,

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:43.127
so keeping them apart.

00:39:43.127 --> 00:39:45.085
So that's why the flu is
seasonal, for example.

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:51.130
So the goal is to reduce the
reproductive number R0, which

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:56.200
is the amount of people
infected by each patient to 1.0

00:39:56.200 --> 00:39:57.550
or lower.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:40:00.040
The current rate is
estimated to be two.

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:05.420
And the sequential infection
interval is about seven days,

00:40:05.420 --> 00:40:09.190
you know, basically, starting
from around when shedding

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:14.800
occurs at five days to when
symptoms start at seven days.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:17.880
So if one present infects
two others in seven days,

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:20.990
that would explain the
doubling time of one week.

00:40:20.990 --> 00:40:25.420
So dropping R0 to zero
would make the doubling time

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:26.080
infinite.

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:28.820
And drops in between
one and two will

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:33.890
increase the doubling time in
proportion to the dropping R0.

00:40:33.890 --> 00:40:39.370
So you know, we have been
able in other countries

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:46.060
to drop the R0 to 1.0, to
a constant very small rate

00:40:46.060 --> 00:40:49.600
of new cases in South
Korea, Taiwan, Singapore,

00:40:49.600 --> 00:40:50.390
and Hong Kong.

00:40:50.390 --> 00:40:52.640
And that's actually without
shutting down the economy.

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:56.560
That's by doing social
distancing, very, very robust

00:40:56.560 --> 00:41:00.920
testing and tracing and very
strict hygiene, especially

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:05.870
the use of face masks and being
very careful what you touch.

00:41:05.870 --> 00:41:09.250
Michael, I wanted to follow
up with a couple of questions

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:10.270
from the chat.

00:41:10.270 --> 00:41:17.110
People are wondering, Wuhan was
very successful in flattening

00:41:17.110 --> 00:41:18.190
their curve.

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:21.970
Why are the rest
of us struggling?

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:24.550
What should we be doing more?

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:27.420
Right, I think it's
too early to say.

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:29.240
You know, in California
we just implemented

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:31.480
the lockdown on Tuesday.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:33.740
It will take--

00:41:33.740 --> 00:41:35.550
And the cases are ramping up.

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:38.130
So a lot of the increase
you see in the case numbers

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:39.960
is just due to testing.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.300
And that's probably
true even in places

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:46.920
like Italy where they've had
a lockdown for a little bit

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.270
longer.

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:53.390
So yeah, you know,
as a reminder,

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:57.080
I was to get infected today,
I wouldn't show symptoms--

00:41:57.080 --> 00:41:59.570
If I was to be infected
today I wouldn't--

00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:01.010
And if I were to
show symptoms, I

00:42:01.010 --> 00:42:04.610
wouldn't show symptoms for
another five to seven days.

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:07.340
And currently there's a
delay in the US testing.

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:10.100
So I probably wouldn't be
tested for another seven days

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:11.323
after that.

00:42:11.323 --> 00:42:12.740
So now there's
this two week delay

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:18.560
between when infections start
and when testing is complete.

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:24.830
So we won't see any change to
the R0 or the doubling time

00:42:24.830 --> 00:42:28.710
at least for another two weeks.

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:32.190
And in fact, from
news reports, it

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:33.690
seems like most
people with symptoms

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:38.400
are being told to stay at
home to see whether they

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:39.390
get better or worse.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:43.490
They're basically not testing
unless they get worse.

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:46.650
And so that means a large
proportion of mild cases

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:48.060
are being missed right now.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:51.930
As testing expands, it
expands to those mild cases

00:42:51.930 --> 00:42:54.300
or expands people who are
just household contacts.

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:59.410
And then that's gonna explode
the number of diagnosed cases.

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:02.100
So that means we're not gonna
see a reduction by looking

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:03.172
at the case numbers.

00:43:03.172 --> 00:43:05.130
The only way we could
see a reduction, I think,

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:06.540
is looking at the death numbers.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:11.900
Because those will
follow the infections

00:43:11.900 --> 00:43:12.860
by three to four weeks.

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:15.650
But that's still a
faster time window

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:21.140
than the ramp-up of the
testing appears will be.

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:25.820
Michael, lots of other questions
and activity on the chat.

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:30.110
Let's see, can you make a
comment about face masks?

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:31.400
Yes, we're getting to it.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:34.730
Everyone in Asia says
you must use face masks.

00:43:34.730 --> 00:43:35.960
It's the only way.

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:40.310
In the US, we're told face
masks don't help at all.

00:43:40.310 --> 00:43:42.950
Yes, I have four
slides on face masks.

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:45.200
OK, we'll wait on that, then.

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:47.600
OK, let's see.

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:49.190
OK, one more question.

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:50.660
What is the best case scenario?

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:53.050
You told us the
worst case scenario.

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:56.490
What is the best case
scenario for recovery?

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:58.750
Yes, in terms of
numbers, I assume?

00:43:58.750 --> 00:43:59.250
Right.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:00.790
I mean, the best case
scenario for a person--

00:44:00.790 --> 00:44:02.090
[INAUDIBLE] in numbers, yeah.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:02.590
Yeah.

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:04.465
Right, so the best-case
scenario for a person

00:44:04.465 --> 00:44:06.480
is asymptomatic disease.

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:11.940
The best scenario for
recovery without vaccines

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:14.832
is probably something
like the next line

00:44:14.832 --> 00:44:16.290
in the slide I was
going to get to,

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:19.385
which is complete
household isolation.

00:44:19.385 --> 00:44:20.610
Like, for the country.

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:22.890
Michael, for the country.

00:44:22.890 --> 00:44:24.060
Oh, for the country.

00:44:24.060 --> 00:44:27.810
What's the best-- let's
say everyone stays at home.

00:44:27.810 --> 00:44:29.720
What's the best-case scenario?

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:33.387
How many more weeks to
recovery from where we are?

00:44:33.387 --> 00:44:35.220
Right, so I think if
everyone stays at home,

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:38.170
it's likely we can limit
the R0 to something like 1.0

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:42.320
on average, maybe even lower.

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:44.930
So in Wuhan, they did
complete household isolation,

00:44:44.930 --> 00:44:49.460
which is similar to what
we're doing now in California,

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:51.320
except they probably
have fewer exceptions

00:44:51.320 --> 00:44:52.250
for vital industries.

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:54.020
So here in California,
we have exceptions

00:44:54.020 --> 00:45:01.400
for a whole slew of essential
industries, including,

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:02.990
marijuana dispensaries.

00:45:06.040 --> 00:45:09.710
But in Wuhan, they had
complete house isolation.

00:45:09.710 --> 00:45:13.550
And, you know, they took
infected household members

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:20.420
to central quarantining
locations or hospitals.

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:22.160
And they practiced
strict hygiene.

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:25.860
And this dropped the
R0 from 3.9 to 0.32.

00:45:25.860 --> 00:45:30.210
And now Wuhan or China is
reporting no new cases.

00:45:30.210 --> 00:45:32.670
Some people doubt
that's actually true.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:34.260
But regardless, they
have a large drop

00:45:34.260 --> 00:45:35.230
in the number of cases.

00:45:35.230 --> 00:45:39.135
So if we practiced complete
house isolation, then,

00:45:39.135 --> 00:45:40.780
you know, the disease
would go away.

00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:45.420
But that would require
complete stoppage

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:46.720
of even essential industries.

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.890
So I don't think that's
likely in the US.

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:53.800
But it's possible we can drop
the R0 to something like one.

00:45:53.800 --> 00:45:58.130
And then once better drugs
or a vaccine comes along,

00:45:58.130 --> 00:45:59.960
then it would be safe.

00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:04.320
The big fear is pre-symptomatic
or asymptomatic transmission.

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:07.520
In Wuhan, when they took
the symptomatic patients out

00:46:07.520 --> 00:46:12.020
of the home, the R0
fell from 1.3 to 0.32.

00:46:12.020 --> 00:46:16.400
So this suggests that only
24%, or that fraction, 0.32

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:20.940
over 1.3, of transmission
events occurred before symptoms.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:25.460
But another study estimates
that 44% of transmission

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:27.410
is in the
pre-symptomatic period.

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:28.470
That's done by modeling.

00:46:28.470 --> 00:46:31.040
So, you know, I'm
not able to evaluate

00:46:31.040 --> 00:46:34.760
those models or the quality of
the data behind those models

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:37.240
so I don't know how
good those numbers are.

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:40.810
But, you know, we can assume
something like 24% to 44%,

00:46:40.810 --> 00:46:43.160
or maybe a third of
transmission events

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:49.710
occur before getting
symptoms, without symptoms.

00:46:49.710 --> 00:46:51.650
So how about completely
asymptomatic patients?

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:56.990
So the undiagnosed carriers
are estimated to be about 50%

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:01.460
as infectious as
the diagnose cases.

00:47:01.460 --> 00:47:04.620
The undiagnosed
carriers in this study,

00:47:04.620 --> 00:47:06.980
which was just published
in Science this week,

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:09.630
include people who will
eventually get the disease.

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:12.440
So that's not
completely asymptomatic.

00:47:12.440 --> 00:47:15.800
But undiagnosed
carriers are more

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:19.490
likely be asymptomatic than
diagnosed ones, obviously.

00:47:19.490 --> 00:47:23.750
And 50% of people
who get the disease

00:47:23.750 --> 00:47:25.790
seem to not have
any symptoms at all.

00:47:25.790 --> 00:47:28.400
So this implies that
asymptomatics are not

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:30.320
as infectious as
symptomatic people.

00:47:32.890 --> 00:47:35.450
So go to the next slide.

00:47:35.450 --> 00:47:37.040
Yeah, so how effective is this?

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:41.630
We talked a bit already
about the China,

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:44.820
South Korea, Taiwan,
Singapore experiences.

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:46.700
But you can see that
here on this graph.

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:49.580
The y-axis is log scale.

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:56.180
So that means a line would
be exponential growth.

00:47:56.180 --> 00:47:58.970
And so you could see that
some countries are still

00:47:58.970 --> 00:48:04.850
on a line, like France, like
Japan, actually, and the US.

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:09.253
But others have become stable.

00:48:09.253 --> 00:48:10.420
Again, this is case numbers.

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:13.400
So some of the initial growth
in cases, especially in the US,

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:14.960
is just from faster testing.

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:20.610
So everyone wants to
know if there are drugs.

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:22.818
So this is the part where
I probably will go quickly.

00:48:22.818 --> 00:48:25.380
Because this is mostly done
for a biochemical audience.

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:29.300
But I'll say that there
are a few drugs that

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:31.670
are very likely to work.

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:34.100
So one is this drug
that's already approved

00:48:34.100 --> 00:48:36.860
in Japan for pancreatitis.

00:48:36.860 --> 00:48:40.010
It's an emitter of a class
of enzymes, proteases,

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:42.776
that are required for
entry of the virus.

00:48:42.776 --> 00:48:47.450
And what's interesting is it
was tested in cell culture.

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:51.200
And it has wide ability
to block the replication

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:53.150
of all sorts of
coronaviruses, including

00:48:53.150 --> 00:48:55.720
SARS-CoV-1 and SARS-CoV-2.

00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:58.400
And that's shown here
in the bottom left.

00:48:58.400 --> 00:49:00.560
It's also been tested
against the original SARS

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:01.460
virus in mice.

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:04.160
So it's always nice to
see things tested in mice.

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:07.910
Because the main barrier
to a drug being effective

00:49:07.910 --> 00:49:11.310
is getting high enough
concentrations in vivo--

00:49:11.310 --> 00:49:13.220
I mean, not having
any toxicity in vivo.

00:49:16.748 --> 00:49:18.290
Things that are
toxic to humans often

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:19.880
show up in toxicity in mice.

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:23.210
So it's a good proxy for humans.

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:29.030
So you can see here that
this drug camostat reduced

00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:35.990
the amount of death from
SARS-CoV-1 infection in mice,

00:49:35.990 --> 00:49:37.430
compared to nothing at all.

00:49:40.410 --> 00:49:43.560
So another one is
this anti flu drug

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:47.370
called Avigan or favipiravir.

00:49:47.370 --> 00:49:51.060
And this is a drug
that gets converted

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:55.050
into a ribonucleoside, which
is the building block of RNA.

00:49:55.050 --> 00:50:00.660
And so then it gets used by
the virus RNA polymerase.

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:06.710
And the virus is now unable
to replicate its genome,

00:50:06.710 --> 00:50:08.810
or at least not able to
replicate it correctly.

00:50:08.810 --> 00:50:11.270
The idea is it creates so many
mutations during replication

00:50:11.270 --> 00:50:15.540
that the virus eventually
fails to replicate.

00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:18.330
So this, again, works
against the current virus

00:50:18.330 --> 00:50:18.960
in human cells.

00:50:22.610 --> 00:50:25.210
And then it actually seems
to have really poor potency.

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:28.930
We use a concentration
required for a 50% inhibition

00:50:28.930 --> 00:50:30.650
as a benchmark for the potency.

00:50:30.650 --> 00:50:32.150
This number is
actually really high.

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:34.340
You usually want it to
be below one micromolar.

00:50:34.340 --> 00:50:36.610
This is 62 micromolars.

00:50:36.610 --> 00:50:38.110
But actually, you
can load this drug

00:50:38.110 --> 00:50:41.260
to a high dose in humans, 400
micromolar in your blood, which

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:43.660
is really high,
with just a pill.

00:50:43.660 --> 00:50:46.090
So this is really efficient.

00:50:46.090 --> 00:50:48.190
And it was declared
two days ago to be

00:50:48.190 --> 00:50:51.220
effective against the
virus in controlled trials

00:50:51.220 --> 00:50:53.650
by Chinese authorities.

00:50:53.650 --> 00:50:55.450
No data was provided,
so we don't really

00:50:55.450 --> 00:50:56.640
know how good the data was.

00:50:56.640 --> 00:50:59.540
But it sounds promising.

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:03.160
This one Kaletra, down here,
is an HIV protease inhibitor.

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:06.530
It was thought that maybe
because the SARS virus also

00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:09.635
uses a protease, a
protease is an enzyme that

00:51:09.635 --> 00:51:10.820
cleaves to other proteins.

00:51:10.820 --> 00:51:14.030
And it's required to process
one big protein in the virus

00:51:14.030 --> 00:51:16.700
to many different
parts, individual parts.

00:51:20.620 --> 00:51:22.520
Sorry, excuse me.

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:26.250
It was thought that maybe
this HIV protease inhibitor

00:51:26.250 --> 00:51:28.140
would be useful.

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:31.800
And there was anecdotal
experience that it was useful.

00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:34.228
But it turned out, just
announced two days ago--

00:51:34.228 --> 00:51:36.020
and I haven't had time
to update the slide,

00:51:36.020 --> 00:51:37.810
and I will right after
the presentation--

00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:39.932
that the controlled
trial failed.

00:51:39.932 --> 00:51:42.390
And so that shows the importance
of doing controlled trials

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:45.035
and not relying on
anecdotal evidence.

00:51:45.035 --> 00:51:49.870
And I always thought that
this rationale was very weak.

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:53.130
So I'm not surprised
that it failed.

00:51:53.130 --> 00:51:55.060
Chloroquine is getting
a lot of press today.

00:51:55.060 --> 00:51:57.150
And I think it's
long overdue press.

00:52:00.330 --> 00:52:03.830
It again has shown to
inhibit viral replication

00:52:03.830 --> 00:52:06.490
of the current virus
in cell culture.

00:52:06.490 --> 00:52:09.900
And a whole month ago, the
Chinese Health Ministry

00:52:09.900 --> 00:52:12.310
said that it was superior
to control treatment

00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:15.510
in multiple trials in China.

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:17.310
But again, no data was shown.

00:52:17.310 --> 00:52:19.730
And actually, I'm not sure
why they don't show data when

00:52:19.730 --> 00:52:22.940
they make these announcements.

00:52:22.940 --> 00:52:25.720
It's a little strange to me.

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:29.550
I actually-- Yeah.

00:52:29.550 --> 00:52:32.770
They're cynical reasons that
they might not be doing it.

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:38.220
Or it could just be
a cultural thing.

00:52:38.220 --> 00:52:40.390
But in any case, they
haven't shown the data,

00:52:40.390 --> 00:52:45.880
but they made the announcement
that it was useful.

00:52:45.880 --> 00:52:51.820
And then just two days ago,
basically, the same conclusion

00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:53.808
was reached by a French group.

00:52:53.808 --> 00:52:55.600
It showed that it was
effective in reducing

00:52:55.600 --> 00:52:59.790
viral titers in patients
in an open-label trial.

00:52:59.790 --> 00:53:03.295
But chloroquine and it's
relative hydroxychloroquine,

00:53:03.295 --> 00:53:04.170
they're both similar.

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:07.290
They're both anti
malarial drugs.

00:53:07.290 --> 00:53:11.430
They do have some side
effects which can be serious.

00:53:11.430 --> 00:53:14.880
But they have been safe to
use for preventing malaria.

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:17.770
You know, I think, you know,
it's funny when the press says,

00:53:17.770 --> 00:53:18.780
oh, it's not proven.

00:53:22.030 --> 00:53:25.137
Yeah, maybe we
shouldn't use it yet.

00:53:25.137 --> 00:53:27.470
I'm like, well, you don't
have to wait for FDA approval.

00:53:27.470 --> 00:53:31.123
It's already a drug that you
give for malaria prevention.

00:53:31.123 --> 00:53:32.540
The current disease
is a lot worse

00:53:32.540 --> 00:53:33.930
than malaria in some respects.

00:53:33.930 --> 00:53:36.138
So I don't see why we wouldn't
just go on and use it.

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:41.472
Another drug that gets a
lot of press is remdesivir.

00:53:41.472 --> 00:53:43.180
And that's because
it's probably the most

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:47.920
specific of the current
antivirals for SARS-CoV-2.

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:50.890
So remdesivir, It's
another prodrug

00:53:50.890 --> 00:53:53.350
that gets converted
into a ribonucleoside

00:53:53.350 --> 00:53:55.695
and is used by the
RNA polymerase.

00:53:55.695 --> 00:53:58.660
It was originally designed
to inhibit the RNA polymerase

00:53:58.660 --> 00:54:02.170
for another RNA virus, Ebola.

00:54:02.170 --> 00:54:04.900
But it was shown in
a published paper

00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:12.760
to inhibit the SARS-CoV-1
RNA polymerase and the virus

00:54:12.760 --> 00:54:15.870
replication in mice
very effectively.

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:21.248
And it also works in human
cells on the current virus.

00:54:21.248 --> 00:54:23.290
And that's not surprising
because the two viruses

00:54:23.290 --> 00:54:27.200
are 96% identical at
the amino acid level.

00:54:27.200 --> 00:54:31.090
So for most purposes, you could
think of the SARS-CoV 1 and 2

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:33.960
biochemically as
essentially the same.

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:37.960
And so now it's being tested
in humans in multiple trials

00:54:37.960 --> 00:54:39.460
in the US and in China.

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:43.720
The China trial data
are due in a few weeks.

00:54:43.720 --> 00:54:47.748
And now it's gotten a lot
of good anecdotal results.

00:54:47.748 --> 00:54:49.290
But again we should
not rely on that.

00:54:49.290 --> 00:54:51.050
That's why we have
the controlled trials.

00:54:54.818 --> 00:54:55.860
So this is a lot of text.

00:54:55.860 --> 00:54:57.380
But basically, I
should just note,

00:54:57.380 --> 00:54:59.750
that they are drugs just to
treat the symptoms and not

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:00.320
the virus.

00:55:00.320 --> 00:55:03.290
So it does this often from
cytokine release syndrome,

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:04.040
as we mentioned.

00:55:04.040 --> 00:55:05.720
And there are drugs for that.

00:55:05.720 --> 00:55:07.470
And so China has
approved one of them,

00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:10.850
which is a Roche antibody
against a cytokine to prevent

00:55:10.850 --> 00:55:13.915
cytokine release syndrome.

00:55:13.915 --> 00:55:16.040
There's poor evidence, but
some evidence that COX-2

00:55:16.040 --> 00:55:17.050
inhibitors--

00:55:17.050 --> 00:55:21.320
that's just Vioxx and
Celebrex which were previously

00:55:21.320 --> 00:55:23.060
prescribed for arthritis--

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:26.470
can prevent cytokine
release syndrome.

00:55:26.470 --> 00:55:30.020
But no one's really
brought that up recently.

00:55:30.020 --> 00:55:31.478
Again, like, treating
this is good,

00:55:31.478 --> 00:55:32.895
but it's not as
good as preventing

00:55:32.895 --> 00:55:34.320
the virus in the first place.

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:36.200
There's this big
raging debate going

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:38.250
on in the medical community
about ACE inhibitors.

00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:40.190
And I'm not going to cover it.

00:55:40.190 --> 00:55:44.240
Because I think it's
really besides the point.

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:46.220
You know, internal
medicine doctors

00:55:46.220 --> 00:55:48.050
love playing with
hypertension drugs.

00:55:48.050 --> 00:55:50.300
And ACE inhibitors are
hypertension drugs.

00:55:50.300 --> 00:55:52.190
You know, some of
you are probably

00:55:52.190 --> 00:55:53.510
on three hypertension drugs.

00:55:53.510 --> 00:55:54.515
I mean, this is just--

00:55:54.515 --> 00:55:57.080
internal medicine doctors
live for doing this stuff.

00:55:57.080 --> 00:56:00.380
And they're fighting
with each other

00:56:00.380 --> 00:56:07.030
over whether having more
or less ACE2 activity is

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:09.070
good for the virus.

00:56:09.070 --> 00:56:11.230
I think it's a side show.

00:56:11.230 --> 00:56:13.870
They're talking about
preventing lung injury

00:56:13.870 --> 00:56:17.840
or decreasing number of
receptors for the virus.

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:19.140
I think it doesn't matter.

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:21.230
I'm not even going
to touch on it.

00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:22.975
So I'm not even gonna cover it.

00:56:22.975 --> 00:56:25.350
But anyway, there's a link
here for more clinical trials,

00:56:25.350 --> 00:56:29.050
if you're interested.

00:56:29.050 --> 00:56:31.280
So there's a whole
bunch of new things.

00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:33.270
You can make antibodies
from patients

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:36.420
or you could screen antibodies
in libraries in vitro

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:41.260
and just produce antibodies
that will bind to the virus.

00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:44.940
So this has already been
announced by two companies Vir

00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:45.840
and Regeneron.

00:56:45.840 --> 00:56:48.120
I'm sure there are many
more companies doing this.

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:51.060
This is a tried and true method.

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:54.510
This is why sometimes you get
a travel shot of immunoglobulin

00:56:54.510 --> 00:56:58.560
before you go traveling
for different viruses.

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:01.600
So it can be quite effective.

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:04.410
And so, you can
also make more drugs

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:09.060
based on the ones that already
know, like that flu drug,

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:11.340
like the remdesivir.

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:13.240
And you can screen for
drugs, either in vitro

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:14.650
or virtually on a computer.

00:57:14.650 --> 00:57:16.963
So all these things
are being done.

00:57:16.963 --> 00:57:18.630
And then, of course,
what we really want

00:57:18.630 --> 00:57:22.120
is a vaccine that we can give
to the entire population.

00:57:22.120 --> 00:57:24.660
And the fastest right now
are these RNA-based vaccines

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:27.860
where they just inject
RNA into your blood.

00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:29.550
And some of it goes
inside your cells

00:57:29.550 --> 00:57:31.350
and your cells will
produce viral proteins.

00:57:31.350 --> 00:57:32.880
And you'll enlist
an immune response

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:35.250
to those viral proteins.

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:36.380
So there's some more--

00:57:36.380 --> 00:57:38.880
there's a nice-- for
chemists, there's

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:43.050
this review of all these
different approaches.

00:57:43.050 --> 00:57:47.160
I wonder why we don't just fix
the virus with formaldehyde

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:49.380
and inject it, which
is how the first polio

00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:52.620
vaccine was made by Jonas Salk.

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:57.480
And a fixed SARS one
vaccine was found

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:59.540
to be protective in monkeys.

00:57:59.540 --> 00:58:01.350
And this has been
proposed for SARS-CoV-2.

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:03.360
It's not like nobody's
thinking about this.

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:06.210
So I'm guessing it's
been done in China.

00:58:06.210 --> 00:58:08.300
It's been proposed in
this article that was

00:58:08.300 --> 00:58:10.350
written by a Chinese scientist.

00:58:10.350 --> 00:58:13.198
So I'm sure it's
being done in China.

00:58:13.198 --> 00:58:15.240
But you don't hear anything
about this in the US.

00:58:15.240 --> 00:58:17.550
You know, the CDC
has its own lab.

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:20.040
And they used to do
their own research.

00:58:20.040 --> 00:58:22.500
I don't know if that's
been cut recently.

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:24.930
But if they're still
doing that, this

00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:27.150
is what they should
be doing, just making

00:58:27.150 --> 00:58:31.560
the fastest, simplest
vaccine and telling us

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:33.960
that they're doing it.

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:36.520
So that gets me to the
next point, which is--

00:58:36.520 --> 00:58:39.450
actually, I should probably
skip the many failures

00:58:39.450 --> 00:58:42.750
of the CDC and the FDA.

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:43.470
It's interesting.

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:44.430
You can read about it.

00:58:44.430 --> 00:58:46.920
I need to look at the time.

00:58:46.920 --> 00:58:48.300
Oh, we're out of time.

00:58:48.300 --> 00:58:49.200
So let's skip this.

00:58:49.200 --> 00:58:51.720
If you guys are interested--

00:58:51.720 --> 00:58:55.110
Michael, we are still
interested and we're still here,

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:58.830
those of us who can
stay online will.

00:58:58.830 --> 00:59:00.750
And you still have
a few minutes.

00:59:00.750 --> 00:59:02.670
We'd love to hear.

00:59:02.670 --> 00:59:06.090
Yeah, we're gonna get to face
masks, which is what was asked.

00:59:06.090 --> 00:59:11.880
So here's a little table
of the symptoms of COVID-19

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:12.880
versus the other thing.

00:59:12.880 --> 00:59:16.080
So it's good, it's
useful for knowing

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:18.150
how it's different from
the cold or allergies.

00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:19.692
But basically, you
don't tend to have

00:59:19.692 --> 00:59:21.480
the runny nose or sneezing.

00:59:21.480 --> 00:59:26.860
But you have all the flu like
symptoms of a fever, cough,

00:59:26.860 --> 00:59:29.880
shortness of breath,
headaches, aches.

00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:33.540
So recommendations for hygiene,
there are many recommendations.

00:59:33.540 --> 00:59:37.630
But essentially,
there are two types.

00:59:37.630 --> 00:59:43.410
So the first type is being
careful to keep the things you

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:46.350
own and your own hands clean.

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:49.090
So I'm a liberal user
of hand sanitizer.

00:59:49.090 --> 00:59:52.530
So the alcohol in hand sanitizer
will kill the bacteria.

00:59:52.530 --> 00:59:55.303
So you hear these things
like, wash your hands often.

00:59:55.303 --> 00:59:56.220
Don't touch your face.

00:59:56.220 --> 00:59:57.250
Like, what does it mean?

00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:58.440
How often is often?

00:59:58.440 --> 01:00:00.300
Why can't I touch my face?

01:00:00.300 --> 01:00:01.830
I'm gonna touch
my face right now.

01:00:01.830 --> 01:00:02.885
It's totally fine.

01:00:02.885 --> 01:00:05.010
Somebody freaked out the
other day when I did that.

01:00:05.010 --> 01:00:05.927
I'm like, I'm at home.

01:00:05.927 --> 01:00:07.170
Everything is clean.

01:00:07.170 --> 01:00:09.750
So basically you should
establish clean zones.

01:00:09.750 --> 01:00:11.910
This is similar to
like, if you ever done

01:00:11.910 --> 01:00:13.860
surgery on an animal
or watched surgery,

01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:16.620
obviously you sanitize
before surgery.

01:00:16.620 --> 01:00:18.320
And then you have a clean zone.

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:20.910
Once everything is
clean, clean things

01:00:20.910 --> 01:00:22.680
can touch other clean things.

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:25.200
So that means that you
should sanitize your hands

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:28.050
right after touching things
that are touched by others.

01:00:30.780 --> 01:00:32.800
You should preferably
only use clean hands

01:00:32.800 --> 01:00:37.760
to touch other people's things
or handing things to others.

01:00:37.760 --> 01:00:39.980
But clean your hands
again afterwards.

01:00:39.980 --> 01:00:42.560
And that means also sanitizing
objects you get, right?

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:46.130
So like, if someone drops
off something for you,

01:00:46.130 --> 01:00:48.710
I would actually sanitize
the outside of the package.

01:00:48.710 --> 01:00:51.300
If it's cardboard, I wouldn't
worry about it too much.

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:53.242
I would just leave
the cardboard there.

01:00:53.242 --> 01:00:54.950
But if they're giving
you a smooth object

01:00:54.950 --> 01:00:59.330
like, you know, yeah,
like a CD, you know,

01:00:59.330 --> 01:01:02.630
something that can hold
the virus, I would wash it.

01:01:02.630 --> 01:01:04.400
And sanitation means
washing with soap

01:01:04.400 --> 01:01:09.440
or using hand sanitizer or
using Windex or using bleach.

01:01:09.440 --> 01:01:11.463
And so again, if
you already have

01:01:11.463 --> 01:01:13.630
something clean in your
house, you don't have worry.

01:01:13.630 --> 01:01:14.980
You don't have to
keep on rewashing it.

01:01:14.980 --> 01:01:16.897
You don't have to keep
on rewashing your hands

01:01:16.897 --> 01:01:18.280
when you're at home.

01:01:18.280 --> 01:01:21.340
Right, so people ask
about disinfectants,

01:01:21.340 --> 01:01:24.910
you know, like Lysol or
things that have bleach.

01:01:24.910 --> 01:01:27.940
These are made for large areas
that you can't really soap

01:01:27.940 --> 01:01:31.640
and wash or put alcohol on.

01:01:31.640 --> 01:01:33.100
If you can use soap
or alcohol, you

01:01:33.100 --> 01:01:35.500
don't need to also
bleach something.

01:01:35.500 --> 01:01:39.140
Like, no one tells you to put
your hands in bleach after you

01:01:39.140 --> 01:01:40.390
wash them with soap and water.

01:01:40.390 --> 01:01:42.700
So if you can wash an
object with soap and water,

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:45.190
you don't need to
quote/unquote disinfect it

01:01:45.190 --> 01:01:48.600
with a disinfectant.

01:01:48.600 --> 01:01:52.670
So I just keep my hands to
myself as much as possible.

01:01:52.670 --> 01:01:56.800
Face masks-- short
answer is yes.

01:01:56.800 --> 01:02:00.640
So the US are fond of saying
that they're ineffective

01:02:00.640 --> 01:02:03.672
or that you shouldn't buy them
because caregivers need them.

01:02:03.672 --> 01:02:06.130
But both of those statements
cannot be true simultaneously.

01:02:06.130 --> 01:02:08.005
If they were ineffective,
then the caregivers

01:02:08.005 --> 01:02:09.190
would not need them.

01:02:09.190 --> 01:02:12.260
So they are effective.

01:02:12.260 --> 01:02:17.200
So they block 95% of
viruses if worn properly.

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:21.850
And that's because viruses
are carried in droplets.

01:02:21.850 --> 01:02:24.850
And basically, the
masks are filters.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:28.210
So, you know, there's a study
done between surgical masks,

01:02:28.210 --> 01:02:34.240
which is the simple, three-ply
masks that look like they're

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:35.830
made out of paper and cloth.

01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:40.660
And then there's the cup like
masks, the N95 respirators.

01:02:40.660 --> 01:02:42.880
The study said they're
actually similarly effective.

01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:48.290
But the N95 respirators are
thought to be more effective.

01:02:48.290 --> 01:02:49.955
So my recommendations
for mask use

01:02:49.955 --> 01:02:51.640
are based on relative risks.

01:02:51.640 --> 01:02:53.650
The reason we've been
told not to use them

01:02:53.650 --> 01:02:55.880
is to save them
for the caregivers.

01:02:55.880 --> 01:03:00.370
And because the risk that
you'll run into somebody who's

01:03:00.370 --> 01:03:05.320
emitting virus in public is very
low, so basically one in 1,000

01:03:05.320 --> 01:03:06.410
at the moment.

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:08.600
So if you're just walking
by yourself in public,

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:11.020
you'd be wasting a mask.

01:03:11.020 --> 01:03:14.220
But I think they would
be useful when you're

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:16.270
in proximity with
strangers, so when you're

01:03:16.270 --> 01:03:19.360
riding in an airplane, a
train, in an Uber or Lyft

01:03:19.360 --> 01:03:22.030
because you don't know if
the driver may be contagious,

01:03:22.030 --> 01:03:26.080
and especially if you're going
to go to a hospital or clinic.

01:03:26.080 --> 01:03:27.530
So--

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:29.620
What about the store?

01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:32.300
Yeah, so it depends on
how crowded the store is.

01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:34.688
I avoid stores, so I don't
know how crowded they are.

01:03:34.688 --> 01:03:36.230
But I think if you're
going to go in,

01:03:36.230 --> 01:03:37.647
I would have a mask ready.

01:03:37.647 --> 01:03:39.230
If you're going to
go to a supermarket

01:03:39.230 --> 01:03:41.510
and you see that you have
to be waiting in line,

01:03:41.510 --> 01:03:43.970
I would put on a mask.

01:03:43.970 --> 01:03:45.650
And then if you're
immunocompromised

01:03:45.650 --> 01:03:49.520
or you're elderly, it's probably
a good idea to put on a mask

01:03:49.520 --> 01:03:51.320
any time you're in
public, just in case

01:03:51.320 --> 01:03:54.410
the guy who's jogging
by you breathes on you.

01:03:58.257 --> 01:04:00.090
So once the infection
rates climb, then it's

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:04.090
useful for everybody in public.

01:04:04.090 --> 01:04:06.220
And then they're
absolutely recommended

01:04:06.220 --> 01:04:11.500
if you're sick because you block
the acceleration of viruses

01:04:11.500 --> 01:04:13.265
from a sick person.

01:04:13.265 --> 01:04:14.890
So again, the only
controversy of masks

01:04:14.890 --> 01:04:16.900
is due to its limited supply.

01:04:16.900 --> 01:04:19.000
But I think people
are wising up to this.

01:04:19.000 --> 01:04:22.180
The former FDA
commissioner Scott Gottlieb

01:04:22.180 --> 01:04:25.690
just tweeted his
support for mask use.

01:04:25.690 --> 01:04:28.060
So I think you can only
deny the facts for so long.

01:04:28.060 --> 01:04:30.400
So this is sort of an example
of public health officials

01:04:30.400 --> 01:04:33.400
giving people
partial information

01:04:33.400 --> 01:04:35.290
that's deliberately misleading.

01:04:35.290 --> 01:04:37.480
I think they've
been disingenuous.

01:04:37.480 --> 01:04:40.690
And it's caused people
to lose trust in them.

01:04:40.690 --> 01:04:42.940
You know, they should
just be honest.

01:04:42.940 --> 01:04:44.320
You know, people are adults.

01:04:44.320 --> 01:04:47.007
If you tell them masks
are effective but the rate

01:04:47.007 --> 01:04:49.090
of infection right now is
so low and we don't have

01:04:49.090 --> 01:04:53.437
enough masks to go around that
we'd like you not to buy them,

01:04:53.437 --> 01:04:54.520
that makes complete sense.

01:04:54.520 --> 01:04:57.580
But telling people you shouldn't
use them because they're not

01:04:57.580 --> 01:05:00.110
effective for you, it's true.

01:05:00.110 --> 01:05:03.183
But it's true only in a
very misleading sense.

01:05:03.183 --> 01:05:04.600
And it doesn't
engender trust when

01:05:04.600 --> 01:05:07.870
people find out the full truth.

01:05:07.870 --> 01:05:09.375
So you just need
some filtration.

01:05:09.375 --> 01:05:11.500
So if you don't have a mask
and the guy next to you

01:05:11.500 --> 01:05:14.968
is coughing, then just put
your nose into your sleeve

01:05:14.968 --> 01:05:16.010
and breathe through that.

01:05:16.010 --> 01:05:18.730
I mean, I do that when I
go to the bathroom anyway.

01:05:18.730 --> 01:05:20.080
It's a really smelly bathroom.

01:05:20.080 --> 01:05:22.883
And you know from experience
that that blocks odors so it

01:05:22.883 --> 01:05:24.050
must be filtering something.

01:05:24.050 --> 01:05:26.840
And in fact, a study shows
that a t-shirt blocks

01:05:26.840 --> 01:05:28.550
70% of virus-sized particles.

01:05:28.550 --> 01:05:31.460
So that makes sense.

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:33.560
So I won't go into
this, how to use masks

01:05:33.560 --> 01:05:34.580
and how to conserve
masks because I

01:05:34.580 --> 01:05:36.650
think most people just want to
know whether it's a good idea

01:05:36.650 --> 01:05:37.280
or not.

01:05:37.280 --> 01:05:41.520
But I would say that in Asia,
like in Taiwan and South Korea,

01:05:41.520 --> 01:05:45.350
China, Japan, it's
not just OK, it's

01:05:45.350 --> 01:05:47.090
expected that you wear masks.

01:05:47.090 --> 01:05:48.770
And in mainland
China, it's required

01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:51.522
that you wear masks in public.

01:05:51.522 --> 01:05:53.480
So even if they have a
very low infection rate,

01:05:53.480 --> 01:05:55.190
they have enough
masks to go around.

01:05:55.190 --> 01:05:56.012
Yes?

01:05:56.012 --> 01:05:57.470
Michael, for what
it's worth, we're

01:05:57.470 --> 01:06:02.600
trying to mobilize our alumni
network to help us with masks.

01:06:02.600 --> 01:06:05.925
Because I think you're right.

01:06:05.925 --> 01:06:06.550
It's important.

01:06:06.550 --> 01:06:09.200
Yeah, I mean, the
US, you know, I mean,

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:12.980
the US is increasing its
manufacturing of masks.

01:06:12.980 --> 01:06:16.310
But it's still slow at the
moment relative to need.

01:06:16.310 --> 01:06:19.820
So the washable masks are
not as good as the N95,

01:06:19.820 --> 01:06:22.040
but they're still good?

01:06:22.040 --> 01:06:28.150
Well, the surgical masks and N95
masks are equally unwashable.

01:06:28.150 --> 01:06:31.010
There's cloth masks that you
can buy, like allergy masks.

01:06:31.010 --> 01:06:33.120
And again, they're probably
better than nothing.

01:06:33.120 --> 01:06:35.330
But they're not as good
as surgical masks, which

01:06:35.330 --> 01:06:38.660
are the three ply, you
know, flexible ones

01:06:38.660 --> 01:06:41.540
or the cup-like N95 masks.

01:06:41.540 --> 01:06:45.440
But on this slide, I
suggest decontaminating it

01:06:45.440 --> 01:06:48.740
by heating it up in
the oven at 70 degrees

01:06:48.740 --> 01:06:51.560
Celsius for 30 minutes
or putting them

01:06:51.560 --> 01:06:52.665
in a UV sterilizer.

01:06:52.665 --> 01:06:54.290
But as we mentioned
before, if you just

01:06:54.290 --> 01:06:58.590
let them sit in a
clean place for a week,

01:06:58.590 --> 01:07:01.830
the virus should be dead.

01:07:01.830 --> 01:07:05.743
So I have a list of things
that I think are OK to do,

01:07:05.743 --> 01:07:07.410
like visiting relatives
if you're really

01:07:07.410 --> 01:07:10.560
careful with the hygiene steps.

01:07:10.560 --> 01:07:12.930
But, you know, I
would definitely

01:07:12.930 --> 01:07:14.340
recommend buying
groceries online

01:07:14.340 --> 01:07:16.592
instead of in a grocery store.

01:07:16.592 --> 01:07:19.050
You know, you can say, well,
the person who picks and packs

01:07:19.050 --> 01:07:20.670
your grocery might be infected.

01:07:20.670 --> 01:07:23.400
But the same is true in a
grocery store, the person who

01:07:23.400 --> 01:07:26.980
stocks the items on the shelf
has the same chance of being

01:07:26.980 --> 01:07:27.480
infected.

01:07:27.480 --> 01:07:30.780
Plus, in a grocery store,
you have 1,000 other shoppers

01:07:30.780 --> 01:07:33.420
going through and then the
cashier has to, you know,

01:07:33.420 --> 01:07:36.060
touch the goods that
1,000 other shoppers have

01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:37.110
selected and touched.

01:07:37.110 --> 01:07:40.920
So it's better just
to reduce the number

01:07:40.920 --> 01:07:44.430
of those interactions and
just buy your groceries online

01:07:44.430 --> 01:07:44.940
if you can.

01:07:48.010 --> 01:07:50.090
But that's about it.

01:07:50.090 --> 01:07:52.030
So you know, travel
if necessary.

01:07:52.030 --> 01:07:57.730
I think if you're armed with
hand sanitizer and masks,

01:07:57.730 --> 01:07:58.380
you can travel.

01:07:58.380 --> 01:08:02.200
Just be careful, you know,
with keeping your hands clean,

01:08:02.200 --> 01:08:05.350
keeping your space clean.

01:08:05.350 --> 01:08:09.010
And I would just end with this,
that because 50% of people

01:08:09.010 --> 01:08:13.270
with virus have no symptoms
but will become immune

01:08:13.270 --> 01:08:15.490
and because that
means 95% of people

01:08:15.490 --> 01:08:18.970
don't go to hospital,
most people who

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:22.840
get the disease will recover
at home in about a week.

01:08:22.840 --> 01:08:24.670
The workforce is not threatened.

01:08:24.670 --> 01:08:28.240
So, you know, there will
be enough farmers and truck

01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:33.700
drivers and store workers to
distribute food and sell food.

01:08:33.700 --> 01:08:37.450
So you don't have to go out
and buy everything in sight.

01:08:37.450 --> 01:08:38.830
So that's about it.

01:08:38.830 --> 01:08:44.780
And in the next slide, as
you guys have seen online,

01:08:44.780 --> 01:08:47.100
there's a recipe for how
to make your own hand

01:08:47.100 --> 01:08:48.960
sanitizer if you're
not able to buy any,

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:54.268
out of just ethanol or
isopropanol and glycerol.

01:08:54.268 --> 01:08:54.810
So that's it.

01:08:54.810 --> 01:08:57.750
So I'm sorry to take so long.

01:08:57.750 --> 01:08:59.990
Michael, this has
been fantastic.

01:08:59.990 --> 01:09:02.729
Thank you so much for
taking time for us.

01:09:02.729 --> 01:09:05.220
I wonder, do you have a couple
of minutes for a few more

01:09:05.220 --> 01:09:06.180
questions?

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:07.630
Sure.

01:09:07.630 --> 01:09:10.109
OK, so we don't want
to impose on your time.

01:09:10.109 --> 01:09:13.788
But we do have a
couple of questions.

01:09:13.788 --> 01:09:15.330
Actually, we have
a lot of questions.

01:09:15.330 --> 01:09:18.410
Let me just start pick on a few.

01:09:18.410 --> 01:09:22.460
Can you talk about the
possibility of reinfection?

01:09:22.460 --> 01:09:24.569
Yeah, you mean like
getting an infected again

01:09:24.569 --> 01:09:25.770
after you've cleared it.

01:09:25.770 --> 01:09:26.490
Yes.

01:09:26.490 --> 01:09:28.410
I think it's small.

01:09:28.410 --> 01:09:30.300
You know, there
was this worry that

01:09:30.300 --> 01:09:34.200
was because a woman had
recovered from the virus

01:09:34.200 --> 01:09:38.700
and then left the hospital
with a negative test

01:09:38.700 --> 01:09:43.050
and then got worse and
then got a positive test.

01:09:43.050 --> 01:09:45.750
And it was taken as evidence
that you could clear the virus

01:09:45.750 --> 01:09:47.670
and get reinfected.

01:09:47.670 --> 01:09:50.055
But it's probably just the
negative test was negative.

01:09:50.055 --> 01:09:53.140
I mean, she probably was
fighting the virus pretty well,

01:09:53.140 --> 01:09:54.390
but then the virus rebounded.

01:09:54.390 --> 01:10:01.155
So it's more a case of just
insufficient immune response

01:10:01.155 --> 01:10:02.280
to the virus to begin with.

01:10:02.280 --> 01:10:05.250
But if she cleared it the second
time and it's really clear,

01:10:05.250 --> 01:10:06.690
then it should be fine.

01:10:06.690 --> 01:10:10.050
Your memory, you know,
your B and T cells

01:10:10.050 --> 01:10:11.220
take a week to ramp up.

01:10:13.830 --> 01:10:18.480
But then your long-lived
memory B cells take something

01:10:18.480 --> 01:10:23.190
like a month to differentiate.

01:10:23.190 --> 01:10:28.230
So it has to do with the
switching of the types of IG,

01:10:28.230 --> 01:10:29.360
immunoglobulin.

01:10:29.360 --> 01:10:33.840
So there might be this window
where the virus can come back.

01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:37.390
But it's less likely that you're
getting reinfected from outside

01:10:37.390 --> 01:10:41.490
as it's just a low-level
infection inside.

01:10:41.490 --> 01:10:46.530
So along similar
lines, what can you

01:10:46.530 --> 01:10:49.440
share about the possibility
of recovering this summer

01:10:49.440 --> 01:10:52.920
and fall, all the
students return to school,

01:10:52.920 --> 01:10:55.470
and then we have a recurrence
that's even worse than what

01:10:55.470 --> 01:10:56.715
we have now?

01:10:56.715 --> 01:10:59.340
Yeah, so I think one thing that
public officials are not saying

01:10:59.340 --> 01:11:02.040
yet-- well, they don't know
what's going to happen--

01:11:02.040 --> 01:11:04.260
is that these kinds
of measures are

01:11:04.260 --> 01:11:05.910
going to have to
be kept in place

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:13.950
until a vaccine or a very, very
effective treatment is made.

01:11:13.950 --> 01:11:18.000
So you know, the RNA
vaccine, so we'll probably

01:11:18.000 --> 01:11:24.930
know if they're
useful in six months.

01:11:24.930 --> 01:11:31.530
But then, you know, it might
take another few months to ramp

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:33.640
up production and distribution.

01:11:33.640 --> 01:11:38.520
So we're not likely to be
in time for a September

01:11:38.520 --> 01:11:40.200
vaccination.

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:44.100
These drugs like chloroquine,
remdesivir, you know,

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:47.460
chloroquine you don't want
to really dose to everyone.

01:11:47.460 --> 01:11:50.630
It's too toxic for that.

01:11:50.630 --> 01:11:52.870
You might dose it to
high-risk populations.

01:11:52.870 --> 01:11:55.130
Remdesivir is likely
to be in short supply.

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:57.920
It's probably only
50% effective anyway.

01:11:57.920 --> 01:12:02.810
And so you don't want to rely
on the drugs too much entirely.

01:12:02.810 --> 01:12:04.830
[INAUDIBLE]

01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:08.540
What are the barriers to
manufacturing these drugs

01:12:08.540 --> 01:12:11.108
faster and in larger quantities?

01:12:11.108 --> 01:12:12.400
Chloroquine there's no barrier.

01:12:12.400 --> 01:12:13.108
It's very simple.

01:12:16.180 --> 01:12:19.530
The favipiravir also
looks pretty simple.

01:12:19.530 --> 01:12:21.270
I'm pretty sure they
can be manufactured

01:12:21.270 --> 01:12:24.750
for millions of people.

01:12:24.750 --> 01:12:27.630
But the side
effects are probably

01:12:27.630 --> 01:12:32.580
such that you don't want to take
it unless you really have to.

01:12:35.490 --> 01:12:38.298
Yeah, and then remdesivir
looks more complicated.

01:12:38.298 --> 01:12:39.840
That's probably
harder to synthesize.

01:12:39.840 --> 01:12:43.470
But Gilead is upping
the production.

01:12:43.470 --> 01:12:44.970
But again, these
drugs are not going

01:12:44.970 --> 01:12:47.320
to cure everyone of disease.

01:12:47.320 --> 01:12:50.970
And so they're more like
hospitalization medicine.

01:12:50.970 --> 01:12:55.500
So they only for the 5% of
people go to the hospital.

01:12:55.500 --> 01:12:58.980
And then maybe only 50%
of those who take the drug

01:12:58.980 --> 01:13:01.560
will still be able
to fight it off.

01:13:01.560 --> 01:13:03.800
And that's just a
guess at the moment.

01:13:03.800 --> 01:13:05.670
It may be 75%.

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:09.900
So it's still going to be
a burden on the hospitals

01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:11.790
unless we continue
to be careful.

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:15.510
What I would like to see
is us returning to work,

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:18.450
but just being more careful
like people are in Asia.

01:13:18.450 --> 01:13:20.550
I think it's a public
education problem.

01:13:20.550 --> 01:13:25.140
You know, in the US, the
local authorities don't even

01:13:25.140 --> 01:13:27.150
bother trying a
course of education

01:13:27.150 --> 01:13:30.430
before a course of lockdown.

01:13:30.430 --> 01:13:32.250
Because they've seen
how people don't

01:13:32.250 --> 01:13:38.100
react to proclamations of
staying away from crowded

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:40.740
places.

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:43.280
But, you know, in
Asia you can do it.

01:13:43.280 --> 01:13:47.610
People are maybe much more just
much more scared of viruses,

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:52.050
more used to working in
tight environments, used

01:13:52.050 --> 01:13:57.570
to things like wearing a face
mask and sanitizing spaces.

01:13:57.570 --> 01:14:02.100
Can you also address briefly
the testing bottleneck and what

01:14:02.100 --> 01:14:05.040
we can do to test more?

01:14:05.040 --> 01:14:08.430
I admire how Korea
tested so many people.

01:14:08.430 --> 01:14:14.780
And they have tests that come
around in 50 minutes, I hear.

01:14:14.780 --> 01:14:17.010
What can we do here?

01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:18.297
Yeah, I don't know.

01:14:18.297 --> 01:14:20.130
Yeah, I'm not sure about
the 50 minute test.

01:14:20.130 --> 01:14:20.700
I really don't know.

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:21.330
Maybe there is.

01:14:21.330 --> 01:14:24.810
But that would be amazing.

01:14:24.810 --> 01:14:26.940
What I saw in
articles about Korea

01:14:26.940 --> 01:14:30.530
is that they, first
of all, the companies

01:14:30.530 --> 01:14:33.390
took their own initiative
to develop tests early.

01:14:33.390 --> 01:14:36.045
And then the health ministry
coordinated with many companies

01:14:36.045 --> 01:14:38.035
to roll out multiple
tests at once

01:14:38.035 --> 01:14:40.410
because they knew it might be
a big thing and they wanted

01:14:40.410 --> 01:14:42.150
to be ready.

01:14:42.150 --> 01:14:45.760
You know, Korea gets a lot
of visitors from China.

01:14:45.760 --> 01:14:50.010
So they probably suspected they
would get a lot of infections

01:14:50.010 --> 01:14:50.940
from China.

01:14:50.940 --> 01:14:53.580
In the end, they got
most of their infections

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:58.190
from one patient, patient
31, who infected people

01:14:58.190 --> 01:15:00.420
through her church.

01:15:00.420 --> 01:15:03.186
So they were ready for that.

01:15:03.186 --> 01:15:06.600
You know, in the US, part
of it is the federal nature

01:15:06.600 --> 01:15:07.490
of the government.

01:15:07.490 --> 01:15:09.750
Korea is still a
relatively small country

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:12.310
of 40 million people.

01:15:12.310 --> 01:15:14.830
OK, so what's funny is
when China locked down

01:15:14.830 --> 01:15:18.900
Wuhan in the province of Hubei,
that was 50 million people.

01:15:18.900 --> 01:15:22.490
And I just read an interview by
Dr. Fauci where he was asked,

01:15:22.490 --> 01:15:23.865
would that ever
happen in the US?

01:15:23.865 --> 01:15:25.282
He was like no,
that's impossible.

01:15:25.282 --> 01:15:26.440
We could never do that.

01:15:26.440 --> 01:15:27.940
So now we've locked
down California.

01:15:27.940 --> 01:15:29.100
That's 40 million people.

01:15:29.100 --> 01:15:30.420
It's the size of Korea.

01:15:34.350 --> 01:15:36.270
So it's possible to do it.

01:15:36.270 --> 01:15:39.780
But in the US we
have 320 million

01:15:39.780 --> 01:15:41.385
and the federal government.

01:15:43.970 --> 01:15:46.530
And as we know, we have a
federal government that's been,

01:15:46.530 --> 01:15:49.170
one, starved of
resources, and two,

01:15:49.170 --> 01:16:00.120
ideologically reluctant to
spend a dime on any public good.

01:16:00.120 --> 01:16:05.652
So it's just a complete failure
of our political system.

01:16:05.652 --> 01:16:07.110
Well, I mean, let's
be honest, it's

01:16:07.110 --> 01:16:12.230
a complete failure of one
set of people in charge.

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:17.270
Michael, you have a lot of
captive computer scientists

01:16:17.270 --> 01:16:19.700
and engineers on this call.

01:16:19.700 --> 01:16:21.590
What can computing
and engineering

01:16:21.590 --> 01:16:25.242
do to help with this
difficult moment in time?

01:16:25.242 --> 01:16:26.450
Yeah, that's a good question.

01:16:26.450 --> 01:16:29.796
Not being a computer
scientist myself,

01:16:29.796 --> 01:16:32.690
you know, I don't
want to give advice.

01:16:32.690 --> 01:16:38.140
I think you guys
will figure it out.

01:16:38.140 --> 01:16:43.302
You know, it's-- you know,
making cheap ventilators is

01:16:43.302 --> 01:16:44.635
certainly one possible solution.

01:16:47.520 --> 01:16:52.690
You know, using computer
science to work with biologists

01:16:52.690 --> 01:16:56.342
to screen for drugs
or analyze genomes

01:16:56.342 --> 01:16:57.550
is another possible solution.

01:17:02.447 --> 01:17:04.030
Yeah, I think talk
to your colleagues.

01:17:04.030 --> 01:17:06.680
Talk to your
colleagues in biology.

01:17:06.680 --> 01:17:08.140
It's harder to do
that now that we

01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:10.960
can't gather in the cafeteria.

01:17:10.960 --> 01:17:19.100
But you know, we'll have a
few weeks of being at home.

01:17:19.100 --> 01:17:23.365
It's possible to urge
your department chairs

01:17:23.365 --> 01:17:26.710
to send messages to
their other colleagues

01:17:26.710 --> 01:17:29.950
in other departments
asking if anyone

01:17:29.950 --> 01:17:33.700
needs any assistance in
their research efforts.

01:17:33.700 --> 01:17:35.890
Michael, thank you so much.

01:17:35.890 --> 01:17:39.970
If you get any
computational challenges,

01:17:39.970 --> 01:17:42.590
please reach out to us.

01:17:42.590 --> 01:17:44.380
We would love to help.

01:17:44.380 --> 01:17:46.720
And we really appreciate
you sharing your time

01:17:46.720 --> 01:17:48.290
and your knowledge with us.

01:17:48.290 --> 01:17:48.790
Thank you.

01:17:48.790 --> 01:17:51.797
I'm gonna clap on
behalf of everyone.

01:17:51.797 --> 01:17:52.630
Thank you very much.

01:17:52.630 --> 01:17:54.170
Yeah, I appreciate your time.

01:17:54.170 --> 01:17:55.390
Thank you so much.

01:17:55.390 --> 01:17:56.710
OK, signing off.

01:17:56.710 --> 01:17:57.500
Thank you.

01:17:57.500 --> 01:17:58.220
Bye, everyone.

01:17:58.220 --> 01:17:59.300
Thank you for joining us.

01:17:59.300 --> 01:18:03.210
I hope you enjoyed the
presentation and you learned--

